from pathlib import Path
b=Path('/home/lazyinspirit/Projects/prestige-math-library/physics');p=b/'items/def-tgrx-ads-regulated-generalized-entropy.md';old=p.read_text();s=old
start=s.index('A future-directed causal curve');end=s.index('\n\nA **boundary region**',start)
s=s[:start]+r'''The model supplies a time orientation on $(\overline M,\overline g)$ compatible with the chosen orientations on its interior and timelike boundary. A future-directed causal curve in $(\overline M,\overline g)$ or $(\mathscr I,\gamma)$ is a locally Lipschitz map $\eta:J\to\overline M$ or $\eta:J\to\mathscr I$, respectively, from a real interval, with a nonzero future-directed tangent almost everywhere and $\overline g(\dot\eta,\dot\eta)\le0$ or $\gamma(\dot\eta,\dot\eta)\le0$. Extensions are geometric: a regular increasing reparameterization is an order-preserving locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism $h:J\to J_0$ onto an interval. A curve is **inextendible** if there are no such $h$, a larger interval $\widetilde J$ containing $J_0$ as a proper subinterval, and a causal curve $\widetilde\eta:\widetilde J\to\overline M$ (or to $\mathscr I$ in the boundary case) with $\widetilde\eta\circ h=\eta$. Thus a parametrization on all of $\mathbb R$ does not by itself make a curve inextendible. A **boundary Cauchy slice** $\mathcal C_{\partial}$ is a smooth embedded spacelike hypersurface in $(\mathscr I,\gamma)$ whose inverse image under every inextendible boundary causal curve consists of exactly one parameter value. A **bulk Cauchy slice** for it is a smooth embedded spacelike $d$-dimensional hypersurface $\Sigma$ of $(\overline M,\overline g)$ with $\partial\Sigma=\mathcal C_{\partial}$ whose inverse image under every inextendible causal curve in $\overline M$ consists of exactly one parameter value. This is explicitly a boundary-inclusive convention: curves may meet or run along $\mathscr I$, and no ordinary Cauchy property for the AdS interior alone is imposed. Existence of these slices is supplied model data, not a theorem about every asymptotically AdS spacetime. For each candidate $X$, the model supplies such a slice $\Sigma_X$ containing $X$; one common slice is allowed.''' +s[end:]
start=s.index('Set $B_A:=');end=s.index('\n\nA named finite-regulator',start)
s=s[:start]+r'''Set $B_A:=\mathcal C_{\partial}\setminus A$, a closed subspace of $\Sigma_X$ since $\mathcal C_{\partial}=\partial\Sigma_X$ is closed and $A$ is open in it. Use the ambient locally finite mod-two relative chain model of [[lem-tgrx-locally-finite-relative-chain-model]]: $C_k^{\mathrm{lf}}(\Sigma_X)$ consists of formal singular-simplex sums whose images are locally finite in $\Sigma_X$; quotient by its subgroup supported in $B_A$, and take the homology of the induced face boundary. The mod-two coefficients mean $1+1=0$. The model supplies a finite fundamental chain $a_X$ for the compact manifold with boundary $\overline A$ and an ambient locally finite fundamental chain $x$ for $X$, with boundaries supported in $\partial A$. Here fundamental means precisely the lemma's local condition: at every interior point of the represented manifold the finite point-local relative chain has the unique nonzero mod-two local homology class. These are supplied chain data; local finiteness is required in the ambient slice, and is not inferred by pushing an intrinsic chain through an arbitrary nonproper inclusion. Define $\mathcal H_A$ by $[x]=[a_X]$ in $H_{d-1}^{\mathrm{lf}}(\Sigma_X,B_A;\mathbb Z_2)$. Equivalently, the model supplies $r_X\in C_d^{\mathrm{lf}}(\Sigma_X)$ with $\partial r_X+a_X+x$ supported in $B_A$; denote this relative chain by $R_X$. The lemma proves the quotient boundary is well-defined, squares to zero and gives exactly this equivalence. Finite chain witnesses remain allowed, and on a compact slice this is the ordinary finite relative homology model. When $R_X$ is represented by an ordinary bulk region, its supplied ambient locally finite fundamental chain is used. A proper compatible locally finite triangulation with the lemma's finite local fundamental-class certificates is a sufficient concrete construction by mod-two face cancellation, not a requirement on the full candidate class. The supplied class $\mathcal X_A$ (possibly empty) includes all this slice and chain data; no candidate, region, chain or triangulation existence theorem is asserted.''' +s[end:]
a='Assign $S_{\\mathrm{gen}}^{\\mathrm{ren}}[X]$ exactly when';pos=s.index(a)
s=s[:pos]+r'''On the optional Einstein–Hilbert area branch, the same fixed scheme also specifies, for each label $\Lambda_n$ and candidate, a dimensionless Borel function $0\le\chi_{\Lambda_n,X}\le1$ with compact support $K_{n,X}$ in $X^\circ:=X\setminus\partial X$. Put $h_X:=g|_{TX^\circ}$, a smooth positive-definite induced metric. Its density is $\mu_{h_X}=\sqrt{\det(h_{ab})}\,|dy^1\cdots dy^{d-1}|$ in a surface chart, as in [[def-riemannian-volume-density]]. The determinant transformation $h_y=J^Th_xJ$ gives the absolute-Jacobian density law. Under Countable Choice, meaning that every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-countable-choice]]), [[prop-riemannian-volume-is-the-radon-measure-of-the-riemannian-density]] supplies the corresponding compact-finite Borel measure $\operatorname{vol}_{h_X}$, independent of charts and orientation. Define the finite regulator-specific physical area by

$$\operatorname{Area}_{\Lambda_n}[X]:=\int_{X^\circ}\chi_{\Lambda_n,X}\,d\operatorname{vol}_{h_X},\qquad 0\le\operatorname{Area}_{\Lambda_n}[X]\le\operatorname{vol}_{h_X}(K_{n,X})<\infty.$$

The bound follows directly from $0\le\chi\le1$ and compact finiteness. Smooth cutoffs give the usual intrinsic integral $\int\chi\mu_{h_X}$; sharp compact Borel cutoffs $\chi=\mathbf1_K$ are included. The measure domain here is the Borel domain, with no implicit completion. The geometric area has units $\mathrm{m^{d-1}}$ in the model's length convention. Countable Choice is carried precisely for this measure/integration interface, not for the chain algebra. With supplied $G_{\Lambda_n}>0$ of units $\mathrm{m^d\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$, this branch sets

$$S_{\mathrm{grav},n}[X]=\frac{k_Bc^3\operatorname{Area}_{\Lambda_n}[X]}{4G_{\Lambda_n}\hbar}+S_{\mathrm{other},n}[X],$$

where $c$ has units $\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $\hbar$ has units $\mathrm{J\,s}$, and the finite $S_{\mathrm{other},n}$ includes the scheme's specified counterterms and finite normalization. The cutoff functions, their relation to $\Lambda_n$, and every subtraction are fixed by that named scheme; no unregulated physical-metric area of a boundary-anchored surface is substituted. General assigned finite $S_{\mathrm{grav},n}$ data outside this optional area branch remain allowed.

''' +s[pos:]
s=s.replace('$V_p\\in(T_pX)^\\perp_g$ and $V=0$ on $\\partial X$','$V_p\\in(T_pX)^\\perp_g$ for interior $p$, and the velocity extends to zero in $T\\overline M$ along $\\partial X$')
s=s.replace('[[def-tgr-regulated-generalized-entropy]]\n\n[Ahmed','[[def-tgr-regulated-generalized-entropy]]\n\n[[lem-tgrx-locally-finite-relative-chain-model]]\n\n[[def-riemannian-volume-density]]\n\n[[prop-riemannian-volume-is-the-radon-measure-of-the-riemannian-density]]\n\n[[def-countable-choice]]\n\n[Ahmed',1)
assert s.split('---',2)[1]==old.split('---',2)[1];p.write_text(s)
p=b/'items/post-tgrx-semiclassical-ads-dictionary.md';old=p.read_text();s=old;a='Choose a smooth spacelike boundary Cauchy slice $\\mathcal C_{\\partial}$, meaning a hypersurface intersected exactly once by each inextendible causal curve of the conformal boundary, and';z='Choose a smooth spacelike boundary Cauchy slice $\\mathcal C_{\\partial}$ in the geometric inextendibility convention of [[def-tgrx-ads-regulated-generalized-entropy]]: increasing regular reparameterizations are allowed when testing extensions, and each maximal boundary causal curve meets it exactly once. Bulk candidate slices use that definition\'s boundary-inclusive Cauchy convention in $(\\overline M,\\overline g)$, with the compatible model-supplied time orientation; no interior-only Cauchy existence is inferred. Choose';assert a in s;s=s.replace(a,z)
a='When the chosen Einstein–Hilbert branch uses the area term, that term is $k_Bc^3\\operatorname{Area}(X)/(4G_{\\Lambda_n}\\hbar)$ with $\\operatorname{Area}(X)$ in $\\mathrm{m^{d-1}}$. Indeed, $[c^3\\operatorname{Area}(X)]=\\mathrm{m^{d+2}\\,s^{-3}}=[G_{\\Lambda_n}\\hbar]$, so the factor multiplying $k_B$ is dimensionless; for $d=3$ these are the usual four-dimensional SI units.'
z='When the chosen Einstein–Hilbert branch uses the area term, use the same label-dependent physical-metric area $\\operatorname{Area}_{\\Lambda_n}[X]=\\int_{X^\\circ}\\chi_{\\Lambda_n,X}\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_{g|_{TX^\\circ}}$ defined by the candidate-surface model, with its explicit Countable Choice integration premise and its fixed finite cutoff data. This term is $k_Bc^3\\operatorname{Area}_{\\Lambda_n}[X]/(4G_{\\Lambda_n}\\hbar)$, and all additional finite terms remain in the prescribed $S_{\\mathrm{other},n}$. The cutoff area is in $\\mathrm{m^{d-1}}$, so $[c^3\\operatorname{Area}_{\\Lambda_n}[X]]=\\mathrm{m^{d+2}\\,s^{-3}}=[G_{\\Lambda_n}\\hbar]$ and the factor multiplying $k_B$ is dimensionless. The metric, Borel cutoff, label dependence and subtraction convention are thus fixed before the combined renormalized limit is tested; an unregulated area at the conformal boundary is not asserted finite. For $d=3$ these are the usual four-dimensional SI units.';assert a in s;s=s.replace(a,z);assert s.split('---',2)[1]==old.split('---',2)[1];p.write_text(s)
print('Applied authorized two body repairs; existing frontmatters unchanged')
